  "notes": "Fix runtime diagnostic output ownership and retain cooler conflict evidence.\n\nIdle smoke-purge servicing no longer writes the cooler output while a runtime preflight owns it. Active/queued purge and generation shutdown remains intact. Critical-output handoff and request-scoped cancellation preserve the reason for interrupted tests; output conflicts remain inconclusive rather than becoming fabricated no-load evidence.\n\nThe connected device now completed the one-second cooler acquisition with nine qualified samples (661.286 mA mean) and a valid LEDC route at 992 ms, instead of aborting after 23 ms. Heater acquisition completed with three samples. This proves acquisition/ownership on that boot, not a fan-health pass: the separate archived-reference model still reports low cooler current (expected 1009.623 mA, tolerance 320 mA), under further investigation. Primary IR and heater-NTC faults also remain; no complete QA success is claimed.\n\nAdded a bounded 48-byte retained volatile conflict record with request ID, duty and raw route registers, captured before cleanup and guarded against cancellation/deadline races. The existing cooler route row is unchanged. No output limit, safety cutoff or current threshold was relaxed. All 64 learned flashlight rows remain unchanged.\n\nValidation: two fresh review cycles, 39 host suites, checked ESP32-S3 build, actual ELF/map deadline audit, exact USB END/hash acknowledgement and normal Desktop/Twin readback. Internal framebuffer and BLE admission policy are unchanged. Firmware: 3165744 bytes; SHA-256 d0e7f5187c59a72d589a18e787204f3f6cca77ab9c99ea532570a4b9a7f2086c.\n",
